eap-peap: detect EAP-Extensions by its full header

In PEAPv0 the phase2 packets have no EAP header, so byte 4 of an
MSCHAPv2 Challenge is the low byte of MS-Length. With a 12-character
server name (as Cisco ISE nodes can have) that is 33, EAP_TYPE_EXTENSIONS,
and the Challenge was rejected as a malformed Extensions packet: the
tunnel was closed without a log line and every login failed.

--- a/src/eap-peap.c
+++ b/src/eap-peap.c
@@ -507,17 +507,15 @@
 	struct peap_state *peap_state;
 	uint8_t id;
 
-	if (len > 4 && pkt[4] == EAP_TYPE_EXTENSIONS) {
-		uint16_t pkt_len;
-		uint8_t code = pkt[0];
-
-		if (code != EAP_CODE_REQUEST)
-			return false;
-
-		pkt_len = l_get_be16(pkt + 2);
-		if (pkt_len != len)
-			return false;
-
+	/*
+	 * PEAPv0 phase2 packets are headerless, so pkt[4] alone can't identify
+	 * an EAP-Extensions packet: in an MSCHAPv2 Challenge it is the low byte
+	 * of MS-Length, which is 33 for a 12-character server name.  Require a
+	 * full header, as wpa_supplicant does, and hand anything else to phase2.
+	 */
+	if (len > 4 && pkt[0] == EAP_CODE_REQUEST &&
+			l_get_be16(pkt + 2) == len &&
+			pkt[4] == EAP_TYPE_EXTENSIONS) {
 		id = pkt[1];
 
 		eap_extensions_handle_request(eap, id,
